course: dictionary
lesson: 15491
title: Proper nouns: Stuart and the Stuarts
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A proper noun is the name of one particular person, place or thing, and it always starts with a capital letter. «Stuart kicked the ball over the fence.» Here Stuart is a person's name, so it has a capital S.
explain: Stuart can also be a family name, and in history it can mean a whole royal family. «The Stuarts were a royal family who ruled Scotland.» Here the Stuarts means the family, not one boy, so it is still a proper noun with a capital letter.
explain: Context clues help you decide which meaning fits. If the sentence is about a person doing something, Stuart is probably a name. If it talks about kings, queens or long ago, it probably means the royal family. Check by putting in the other meaning and seeing if the sentence still makes sense.
fact: A proper noun is the name of a particular person, place or thing, and it starts with a capital letter.
fact: «Stuart» is a boy's first name and also a family name, used especially in Scotland and England.
fact: «The Stuarts» were a royal family who ruled Scotland, and later England and Ireland too, from 1603 to 1714.
fact: Kings from this family included James I, Charles I, Charles II and Queen Anne.
fact: The older Scottish spelling of the royal family is «Stewart», so the same family can be written two ways.
exercise: «Stuart kicked the ball over the fence.» What is Stuart in this sentence? | A place where you can go on holiday | A type of ball | *A person's name | A colour
exercise: «The Stuarts were a royal family who ruled Scotland.» What do the Stuarts mean here? | A type of Scottish dinner | A group of school children | A stone castle on a hill | *A royal family from history
exercise: Why does «Stuart» start with a capital letter when it is a name? | It starts every sentence, always | *It is a proper noun, the name of a particular person | It is a long word | It is a very important word
test: Which king was part of the Stuart royal family? | Henry VIII | *Charles I | Richard III | Alfred the Great
test: «The Great Fire of London happened in Stuart times.» What does Stuart times mean? | *The time when the Stuart royal family ruled | Times when everyone wears costumes | A time before the Stuarts were born | A time of year when it snows
exam: The Stuarts ruled from 1603 to 1714, so when did their rule begin? | *1603 | 1485 | 1666 | 1066
